---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';

// Copy is an accurate technical description written against AGENTS.md and
// DECISIONS.md, claim-verified against source 2026-08-10 — NOT legally
// reviewed (STATUS: lawyer's read owed before launch). Every claim here is
// checkable; two were repaired during verification (probing on select,
// rate-counter contents). The no-analytics sentence is GUARDED by doc-sync
// (layer 5): adding an analytics script while it stands fails the suite.
//
// Last-updated derives from git at BUILD TIME so it cannot rot silently:
// the date is this file's last commit date, injected by astro.config.mjs
// as a define — the Cloudflare adapter prerenders in WORKERD, so
// node:child_process cannot run here; the config runs in Node and can.
const lastUpdated = __PRIVACY_LAST_UPDATED__;
const dateHuman = new Date(`${lastUpdated}T00:00:00Z`).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', {
  day: 'numeric',
  month: 'long',
  year: 'numeric',
  timeZone: 'UTC',
});
---

<Layout
	title="Privacy — ImageExtract"
	description="ImageExtract's privacy policy: no accounts, no cookies, no stored URLs or images — what actually happens when you scan a page, and what is briefly held in memory."
>
	<SiteHeader anchorPrefix="/" nav={false} />
	<main class="mx-auto max-w-content px-md py-xl">
		<article class="privacy-article">
			<h1 class="text-h1 font-semibold text-text">Privacy</h1>
			<p class="mt-md text-body text-muted">
				ImageExtract doesn't have accounts, doesn't set cookies, and doesn't store the pages you
				scan or the images you download.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				This page explains what actually happens when you use it.
			</p>

			<h2 class="mt-xl text-h3 font-semibold text-text">What happens when you scan a page</h2>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				You paste a URL. Our server fetches that page, reads its HTML to find image references,
				and sends you back a list. The page's HTML is parsed in memory and discarded when the
				request ends. The URL you entered is never written anywhere.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				The list of images appears in your browser. Filtering, sorting, and searching happen on
				your device and never reach our server. So does selecting — though checking a selected
				image's size and dimensions reads the first few kilobytes of it through our server, which
				streams through and is forgotten like everything else.
			</p>

			<h2 class="mt-xl text-h3 font-semibold text-text">What happens to the images</h2>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				Thumbnails load directly from the site you scanned to your browser. They don't pass
				through us at all.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				Images pass through our server in two cases: when a preview fails to load directly and we
				retry it, and when you download something. In both cases the bytes stream through and are
				forgotten — nothing is written to disk, no cache, no database.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				ZIP archives are assembled in your browser, not on our server.
			</p>

			<h2 class="mt-xl text-h3 font-semibold text-text">What we don't keep</h2>
			<ul class="mt-sm list-disc pl-lg text-body text-muted">
				<li>No accounts, no sign-in, no profiles</li>
				<li class="mt-xs">No record of URLs you scan or images you download</li>
				<li class="mt-xs">No cookies set by us</li>
				<li class="mt-xs">No analytics that identify you</li>
				<li class="mt-xs">No logs containing page URLs or image URLs</li>
			</ul>

			<h2 class="mt-xl text-h3 font-semibold text-text">What is temporarily held</h2>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				Two things exist in server memory and disappear on their own:
			</p>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				<strong class="text-text">Rate-limit counters.</strong> To keep the service usable, we
				count requests per network connection for an hour at a time. These counters hold a
				network address and request counts — nothing about which pages or images were requested.
				They live in memory only and vanish when the server process recycles.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				<strong class="text-text">A DNS cache.</strong> Hostnames are resolved before fetching,
				and results are cached briefly to avoid repeated lookups. Hostnames only, for about a
				minute.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				Neither is written to storage and neither survives a restart.
			</p>

			<h2 class="mt-xl text-h3 font-semibold text-text">Third parties</h2>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				<strong class="text-text">Cloudflare</strong> hosts this service and handles every
				request as our infrastructure provider. Their network sees traffic in transit and they
				may retain logs under their own policies, which we do not control.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				<strong class="text-text">The sites you scan</strong> see a request from us, identified
				as ImageExtract — see <a href="/traffic" class="text-accent hover:underline">/traffic</a>
				for exactly what that request does. When thumbnails load directly from those sites, they
				see your browser as they would any visitor, though we ask your browser not to tell them
				which page you were scanning.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				We use no analytics, advertising, or tracking services.
			</p>

			<h2 class="mt-xl text-h3 font-semibold text-text">Your rights</h2>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				We hold nothing about you, so there is nothing to request, correct, or delete. If you
				believe otherwise, write to
				<strong class="text-text">privacy@imageextract.pics</strong>.
			</p>

			<h2 class="mt-xl text-h3 font-semibold text-text">Changes</h2>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				If this policy changes, the date below changes with it.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-sm mb-xl text-caption text-muted">Last updated: {dateHuman}</p>
		</article>
	</main>
</Layout>

<style>
	/* Reading measure: half the content width, derived from the container
	   token rather than an invented value (the .hero-form precedent). */
	.privacy-article {
		max-width: calc(var(--container-content) / 2);
	}
</style>
